{
  "name": "Prism Fan",
  "notes": "`20-prism` with five clocks instead of one. Each line gets its own sine LFO at the same rate and a phase a fifth of a turn ahead of the one before it -- M0..M4 at 0.0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8 driving L0..L4 in order -- so the five sweeps run as one travelling wave rather than in unison. Identical rates are what keeps the stagger: the offsets are set once and never drift, because nothing pulls the rates apart.\n\nThe sweep is a `translate` warp, not the emitter's `centre`, and that is the load-bearing choice. `offset` has a base of ZERO, and a `multiply` row does `*= (1 + v * depth)` -- so a row multiplying an offset scales the swing and leaves the line's home alone. Multiplying `centre` would have dragged all five toward p=0 instead. The emitter centre is now only where a line lives; the warp is how far it travels.\n\n**A matrix row's `depth` has no address**, so no rack knob can reach the five of them, and `Macro::MAX_TARGETS` is 4 in any case -- the same wall that left `20-prism` with one knob. `M6` is the way round it: a `macro` signal is a plain 0..1 matrix SOURCE, so one rack control fans out to five multiply rows through a single target. The LFO rows carry 0.12 and the multiply rows 2.75, which puts the swing at 0.12 with the knob down and 0.45 -- `20-prism`'s figure -- with it up. Unity at zero is inherent to `1 + v*depth`, so a knob like this scales an amplitude the document chose; it cannot start one from nothing.\n\nAnd because the knob is a modulator parameter rather than a rack entry, the audio band can drive it: `M5` adds bass onto `M6.knob` and the whole fan opens on the beat, one knob upstream of five rows. A row reads its source before a later row writes it, so the bass lands a frame late -- 5 ms at 200 FPS.\n\n`screen` rather than `add`: five saturated lines at value 2.0 crossing under `add` spend their time clipped white, and the crossings are the point.",
